Questions:
{
  "questions": [
    {
      "source_id": "1",
      "stem": "A survey drone is launched from a base at the origin. At a certain moment its position vector is $\\vec r = 8\\hat{i} - 15\\hat{j}$, where distances are measured in kilometers. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The distance between the drone and the base is $23\\ \\text{km}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This adds the component magnitudes ($8+15$) as if distance were Manhattan distance. The straight-line distance should be found from the Euclidean magnitude $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The distance between the drone and the base is $15\\ \\text{km}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly uses only the $y$-component and ignores the $x$-component. The distance from the origin depends on both components via $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The distance between the drone and the base is $17\\ \\text{km}$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The distance from the origin is the magnitude of the position vector: $|\\vec r|=\\sqrt{(8)^2+(-15)^2}$. Computing gives $|\\vec r|=\\sqrt{64+225}=\\sqrt{289}=17\\ \\text{km}$, so this statement is correct."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The distance between the drone and the base is $8\\ \\text{km}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly uses only the $x$-component and ignores the $y$-component. The distance from the origin is the vector magnitude, not a single component."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "2",
      "stem": "A cart moves along the $x$-axis with displacement given by $\\vec s(t)=\\big((2.2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^3})t^3+(1.6\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2})t^2\\big)\\hat{i}$. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "This is a constant-acceleration motion.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Taking two derivatives gives $\\vec a(t)=(13.2t+3.2)\\hat i$, which depends on time. Since $\\vec a$ is not constant, the motion is not constant-acceleration motion."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0$ is $3.2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Differentiate to get velocity $\\vec v(t)=\\frac{d\\vec s}{dt}=(6.6t^2+3.2t)\\hat i$ and acceleration $\\vec a(t)=\\frac{d\\vec v}{dt}=(13.2t+3.2)\\hat i$. Evaluating at $t=0$ gives $|\\vec a(0)|=3.2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration is $1.6\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly reads a coefficient from the displacement function as the acceleration. Acceleration must be found via $\\vec a(t)=\\frac{d^2\\vec s}{dt^2}$, not by inspection of the $t^2$ term."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0$ is $13.2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses the coefficient of $t$ in $\\vec a(t)=(13.2t+3.2)\\hat i$ with the value at $t=0$. Substituting $t=0$ leaves only the constant term $3.2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "3",
      "stem": "An object moves in the plane from position $A$ with $\\vec r_A=-7.0\\hat{i}+4.0\\hat{j}$ to position $B$ with $\\vec r_B=5.0\\hat{i}-2.0\\hat{j}$ in $4.0\\ \\text{s}$. Coordinates are in meters. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The straight-line distance between $A$ and $B$ is about $13.4\\ \\text{m}$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The displacement is $\\Delta\\vec r=\\vec r_B-\\vec r_A=(5-(-7))\\hat i+(-2-4)\\hat j=12\\hat i-6\\hat j$. Its magnitude is $|\\Delta\\vec r|=\\sqrt{12^2+(-6)^2}=\\sqrt{180}\\approx 13.4\\,\\text{m}$, so this statement is correct."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The speed at $t=0$ must be $3.4\\ \\text{m/s}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This assumes the initial speed must equal the average speed $|\\Delta\\vec r|/\\Delta t$. With only start/end positions and total time, infinitely many motions are possible, so the initial speed is not uniquely determined."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The acceleration at $t=0$ must be $0.84\\ \\text{m/s}^2$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This effectively invents an acceleration using a relation like $a\\sim |\\Delta\\vec r|/t^2$ without justification. Acceleration cannot be determined from just two position vectors and an elapsed time unless additional constraints (e.g., constant acceleration plus more information) are provided."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Option A is correct because the magnitude of the displacement between the two given coordinates is about $13.4\\,\\text{m}$. Therefore it is not true that none of the statements is correct."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

Create a complete LaTeX document with:
1. A document preamble with necessary packages
2. Custom macros for question formatting (\QBlock, \Correct, \Wrong)
3. All questions formatted using the macros
4. Proper math formatting for all expressions

Set filename to "isomorphic_questions.tex"

output.latex_content:
% ===== Document Preamble =====
\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=0.5in]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}
\usepackage{xcolor}
\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage{needspace}
\usepackage{comment}

% ===== Colors =====
\definecolor{AnsRed}{RGB}{170,0,0}
\definecolor{WrongBlue}{RGB}{0,0,160}

% ===== Choice list styling (tight, consistent) =====
\setlist[enumerate]{%
  leftmargin=1.5em,
  itemsep=0.25em,
  topsep=0.25em,
  label=\textbf{\Alph*.}
}

% ===== Style Wrappers =====
\newcommand{\QuestionStyle}[1]{{\large\bfseries\boldmath #1}}
\newcommand{\AnswerStyle}[1]{{\large\bfseries\boldmath #1}}
\newcommand{\ReasonStyle}[1]{{\normalsize #1}}

% ===== Show / Hide Reasoning Toggle =====
\newif\ifshowreason
\showreasontrue      % Instructor version
% \showreasonfalse   % Student version

% ===== Choice Item Macros (Answer + Reasoning) =====
% Usage:
%   \Correct{Answer}{Reasoning}
%   \Wrong{Answer}{Reasoning}

\newcommand{\Correct}[2]{%
  \item \AnswerStyle{\textcolor{AnsRed}{#1}}%
  \ifshowreason\\[-0.2em]
    \ReasonStyle{\textcolor{AnsRed}{#2}}%
  \fi
}

\newcommand{\Wrong}[2]{%
  \item \AnswerStyle{\textcolor{WrongBlue}{#1}}%
  \ifshowreason\\[-0.2em]
    \ReasonStyle{\textcolor{WrongBlue}{#2}}%
  \fi
}

% ===== Question Block Macro =====
% Usage: \QBlock{Q1}{stem...}{<items>}

\newcommand{\QBlock}[3]{%
  \Needspace{8\baselineskip}%
  \noindent
  \begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
    \QuestionStyle{#1.~#2}

    \vspace{0.25em}
    \begin{enumerate}
      #3
    \end{enumerate}

    \vspace{0.25em}
    \hrule height 0.4pt
  \end{minipage}
  \vspace{0.75em}
}

% ===== Document Start =====

\begin{document}

\begin{center}
{\huge \textbf{Isomorphic Questions: Vectors and Kinematics}}\\[0.25em]
{\Large Position vectors, displacement, velocity, and acceleration}\\[0.25em]
{\large Multiple choice questions generated below. Answers in RED and incorrect answers in BLUE.}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.75em}

\QBlock{Q1}{A survey drone is launched from a base at the origin. At a certain moment its position vector is $\vec r = 8\hat{i} - 15\hat{j}$, where distances are measured in kilometers. Which statement is correct?}{
  \Wrong{The distance between the drone and the base is $23\ \text{km}$.}{This adds the component magnitudes ($8+15$) as if distance were Manhattan distance. The straight-line distance should be found from the Euclidean magnitude $\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$.}
  \Wrong{The distance between the drone and the base is $15\ \text{km}$.}{This incorrectly uses only the $y$-component and ignores the $x$-component. The distance from the origin depends on both components via $\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$.}
  \Correct{The distance between the drone and the base is $17\ \text{km}$.}{The distance from the origin is the magnitude of the position vector: $|\vec r|=\sqrt{(8)^2+(-15)^2}$. Computing gives $|\vec r|=\sqrt{64+225}=\sqrt{289}=17\ \text{km}$, so this statement is correct.}
  \Wrong{The distance between the drone and the base is $8\ \text{km}$.}{This incorrectly uses only the $x$-component and ignores the $y$-component. The distance from the origin is the vector magnitude, not a single component.}
}

\QBlock{Q2}{A cart moves along the $x$-axis with displacement given by $\vec s(t)=\big((2.2\,\mathrm{m/s^3})t^3+(1.6\,\mathrm{m/s^2})t^2\big)\hat{i}$. Which statement is correct?}{
  \Wrong{This is a constant-acceleration motion.}{Taking two derivatives gives $\vec a(t)=(13.2t+3.2)\hat i$, which depends on time. Since $\vec a$ is not constant, the motion is not constant-acceleration motion.}
  \Correct{The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0$ is $3.2\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$.}{Differentiate to get velocity $\vec v(t)=\frac{d\vec s}{dt}=(6.6t^2+3.2t)\hat i$ and acceleration $\vec a(t)=\frac{d\vec v}{dt}=(13.2t+3.2)\hat i$. Evaluating at $t=0$ gives $|\vec a(0)|=3.2\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$.}
  \Wrong{The magnitude of the acceleration is $1.6\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$.}{This incorrectly reads a coefficient from the displacement function as the acceleration. Acceleration must be found via $\vec a(t)=\frac{d^2\vec s}{dt^2}$, not by inspection of the $t^2$ term.}
  \Wrong{The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0$ is $13.2\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$.}{This confuses the coefficient of $t$ in $\vec a(t)=(13.2t+3.2)\hat i$ with the value at $t=0$. Substituting $t=0$ leaves only the constant term $3.2\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$.}
}

\QBlock{Q3}{An object moves in the plane from position $A$ with $\vec r_A=-7.0\hat{i}+4.0\hat{j}$ to position $B$ with $\vec r_B=5.0\hat{i}-2.0\hat{j}$ in $4.0\ \text{s}$. Coordinates are in meters. Which statement is correct?}{
  \Correct{The straight-line distance between $A$ and $B$ is about $13.4\ \text{m}$.}{The displacement is $\Delta\vec r=\vec r_B-\vec r_A=(5-(-7))\hat i+(-2-4)\hat j=12\hat i-6\hat j$. Its magnitude is $|\Delta\vec r|=\sqrt{12^2+(-6)^2}=\sqrt{180}\approx 13.4\,\text{m}$, so this statement is correct.}
  \Wrong{The speed at $t=0$ must be $3.4\ \text{m/s}$.}{This assumes the initial speed must equal the average speed $|\Delta\vec r|/\Delta t$. With only start/end positions and total time, infinitely many motions are possible, so the initial speed is not uniquely determined.}
  \Wrong{The acceleration at $t=0$ must be $0.84\ \text{m/s}^2$.}{This effectively invents an acceleration using a relation like $a\sim |\Delta\vec r|/t^2$ without justification. Acceleration cannot be determined from just two position vectors and an elapsed time unless additional constraints (e.g., constant acceleration plus more information) are provided.}
  \Wrong{None of the above is correct.}{Option A is correct because the magnitude of the displacement between the two given coordinates is about $13.4\,\text{m}$. Therefore it is not true that none of the statements is correct.}
}

\end{document}
